>Neuter</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="21036_2632843_1795243"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">A company that is accredited to sell Internet domain names.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="2632843">registrar</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">NotSelected</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="nn-NO"><ntig><termGrp><term id="1795243">domeneregistrator</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">NOR</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Masculine</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="21036_763984_1795243"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">A company that is accredited to sell Internet domain names.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="763984">domain registrar</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">NotSelected</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="nn-NO"><ntig><termGrp><term id="1795243">domeneregistrator</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">NOR</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Masculine</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="2104_269344_631544"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">An encryption algorithm system included as part of the 802.11 standard, developed by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers as a security measure to protect wireless LANs from casual eavesdropping. WEP uses a shared secret key to encrypt packets before transmission between wireless LAN devices and monitors packets in transit to detect attempts at modification. WEP offers both 40-bit and 128-bit hardware-based encryption options.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="269344">Wired Equivalent Privacy</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Mass</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="nn-NO"><ntig><termGrp><term id="631544">Wired Equivalent Privacy</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">NOR</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Masculine</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="2104_269344_631545"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">An encryption algorithm system included as part of the 802.11 standard, developed by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers as a security measure to protect wireless LANs from casual eavesdropping. WEP uses a shared secret key to encrypt packets before transmission between wireless LAN devices and monitors packets in transit to detect attempts at modification. WEP offers both 40-bit and 128-bit hardware-based encryption options.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="269344">Wired Equivalent Privacy</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Mass</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="nn-NO"><ntig><termGrp><term id="631545">WEP</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">NOR</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Masculine</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="2104_269345_631544"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">An encryption algorithm system included as part of the 802.11 standard, developed by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers as a security measure to protect wireless LANs from casual eavesdropping. WEP uses a shared secret key to encrypt packets before transmission between wireless LAN devices and monitors packets in transit to detect attempts at modification. WEP offers both 40-bit and 128-bit hardware-based encryption options.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="269345">WEP</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Mass</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="nn-NO"><ntig><termGrp><term id="631544">Wired Equivalent Privacy</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">NOR</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Masculine</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="2104_269345_631545"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">An encryption algorithm system included as part of the 802.11 standard, developed by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers as a security measure to protect wireless LANs from casual eavesdropping. WEP uses a shared secret key to encrypt packets before transmission between wireless LAN devices and monitors packets in transit to detect attempts at modification. WEP offers both 40-bit and 128-bit hardware-based encryption options.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="269345">WEP</term><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Mass</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet><langSet xml:lang="nn-NO"><ntig><termGrp><term id="631545">WEP</term><termNote type="geographicalUsage">NOR</termNote><termNote type="partOfSpeech">Noun</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalGender">Masculine</termNote><termNote type="grammaticalNumber">Singular</termNote></termGrp></ntig></langSet></termEntry><termEntry id="2105_545690_1981077"><langSet xml:lang="en-US"><descripGrp><descrip type="definition">A view that shows text formatting and a simplified page layout.</descrip></descripGrp><ntig><termGrp><term id="545690">draft view</term